      "name": "Inner thigh nerve pain (obturator nerve)",
      "clin": "Obturator nerve entrapment or mechanosensitivity (exercise-induced medial thigh pain)",
      "blurb": "A nerve runs from the low back through the pelvis to the inner thigh, where it supplies the muscles that pull the legs together and a patch of skin on the inner thigh. In some active people, often footballers and runners, it can be pinched in the tissue at the top of the inner thigh, giving a deep ache in the groin that spreads down the inner thigh with exercise, sometimes with tingling or a feeling of weakness. It can also follow pelvic surgery or childbirth.",
