      "id": "myofascial",
      "name": "Muscle-referred neck and shoulder ache (myofascial pain)",
      "clin": "Myofascial pain syndrome: upper trapezius, levator scapulae, suboccipitals (referral to the head and shoulder)",
      "blurb": "A muscle that has become tight and sensitive, often from being held in one position or worked harder than usual, can send a deep ache to a nearby area, such as from the top of the shoulder or side of the neck to the temple or the back of the head. That spreading ache is a normal trick of the nervous system, usually not a trapped nerve or a damaged joint. A tender \"knot\" is not a tangle or a tear: it is thought to be a patch of muscle held tense to protect you, and it can be taught to let go again. It is common, not dangerous, and usually settles once the muscle is moved, loaded gradually, and the posture or habit that overloaded it is changed.",
