      "clin": "Scapular winging: serratus anterior (long thoracic nerve) or trapezius (spinal accessory nerve) weakness",
      "blurb": "Sometimes a nerve to one of the muscles that holds the shoulder blade flat against the ribs is stretched, pressed or inflamed, for example after a viral illness, a heavy backpack, an operation near the neck or chest, or a lot of overhead work. The shoulder blade then lifts away from the back, most clearly when you push or lift the arm forward, and the arm tires and feels weak. Nerves often recover by themselves over months, and physiotherapy can help keep the shoulder moving and strong while that happens.",
